ore public coverage, but none of it separates the rungs on that side. Defending coverage people already have doesn't show \"everyone\" covered (rung 2), an expansion of programmes (rung 3), or full public payment (rung 1).\n- **HB 1472 (2023):** he authored it. I coded it as a record but `adjacent`, because it sets equal hospital reimbursement rates for all payers. That is cost control, not a decision about whom government covers.\n- **Work requirements:** his September 2026 opposition to the Healthy Indiana Plan (HIP) work requirements is `adjacent`, as the annex rules for work requirements on their own.\n- **Campaign remarks:** his remarks at a primary forum and to two news outlets are either `rhetorical` (health care as a goal, with no policy in it) or off the question.\n\n**Still needed (`needs_source`):**\n- The House roll call for SEA 1 (2026), with his vote.\n- Any bill he authored or co-authored on Medicaid eligibility, a state public option, or single-payer coverage.\n\nI coded four candidate quotes. None is rankable or quotable: each is a shared goal, a look back, or off the question.","ttft_ms":34158,"type":"result","duration_ms":67841,"uuid":"e5a525d5-45ee-45a3-add9-d30518484238","ttft_stream_ms":2002,"time_to_request_ms":129,"first_content_frame_ms":2002,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
